WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.960
The Terminator has spawned multiple sequels
and prequels both in movie theaters and on

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.120
television, but as good as it is, it's far
from a perfect film.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:13.320
To learn how, among other things, a perfectly
logical thinking machine acts like a moron,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.460
keep watching for the dumb things everyone
ignored in The Terminator.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.280
Since he's governed by a ruthlessly calculating
artificial intelligence, you might assume

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.940
that the Terminator would make purely logical
choices.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.040
Watch the movie, though, and one of the first
things you'll notice is that his actions absolutely

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.080
do not reflect that.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.660
For one thing, he can't even decide how quiet
he needs to keep his time-travel murder mission.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.620
As soon as he arrives in the '80s, for example,
the Terminator makes an immediate effort to

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.670
get era-appropriate clothing that will allow
him to blend in to 1984 or at least blend

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.980
in as much as an insanely jacked Austrian
bodybuilder can, anyway.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.340
"I think this guy’s a couple cans short
of a six-pack."

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:50.640
"Your clothes.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.520
give them to me, now."

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.220
And yet, throughout the movie, he draws plenty
of attention to himself and never seems to

00:00:58.220 --> 00:00:58.720
care.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.820
He steals a pile of guns from a gun shop and
murders the owner with a shotgun.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.780
This is the loudest possible way he could
do it, which will almost certainly draw the

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.180
attention of the police.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.780
Even in what we see in the movie up to that
point, it's obvious that he could totally

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:15.920
kill the owner with his bare hands, flip the
"closed" sign over, and lock the door behind

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.360
him, giving him at a few least hours before
anyone thinks to call the authorities.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.160
Shortly afterward, he kills the first Sarah
Connor he finds in the phone book.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.350
He bothers to knock on the door and ask her
name, then smashes the door open and shoots

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:29.680
her six times in a quiet residential neighborhood.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.740
As with the gun shop, he could've done it
all much more quietly.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.040
And if he's not going to be quiet, why bother
knocking on the door and asking her name?

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:37.760
Manners?

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.579
Every now and then, you come across a character
in a movie who is so annoying and stupid that

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:47.029
you can't help but despise them as a viewer,
while the comparatively intelligent protagonist

00:01:47.029 --> 00:01:49.580
doesn't seem to thoroughly hate them as much
as they should.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.880
Such is the case of Nancy, the waitress who
ranks among the most frustrating characters

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.060
in the history of film.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.500
Sarah Connor is working at a diner when we
meet her, and not only is her section is filled

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:03.909
with increasingly irate customers, a young
boy takes the opportunity to drop a lump of

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:05.080
ice cream in her apron.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.080
That's when Nancy comes up behind her and
says that nobody's going to care in a hundred

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.010
years, which is the kind of contrived zinger
that she would only say if she knew she was

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.260
in a movie where time traveling robots exterminated
human civilization in the next century.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:21.580
Then, she just bails, without even bothering
to help her friend and co-worker clean up

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.160
the mess.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.540
That might not seem that bad, but don't worry:
Nancy goes completely off the rails a few

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:27.240
minutes later.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.659
After the Terminator kills another woman named
Sarah Connor and it makes the news, Nancy

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:35.810
grabs Sarah and pulls her into the break room
to watch the report, giving her the most insane

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:36.960
reason ever spoken.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.540
"You are not gonna believe this you’re gonna
love this!"

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.620
Why would anyone love hearing about how someone
with their name was violently murdered?

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:45.940
What is wrong with you, Nancy?

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.180
She's just lucky she didn't say this stuff
to super buff T2 Sarah Connor, who would've

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.060
absolutely punched her lights out.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.340
From what Kyle Reese says, he didn't have
a lot of time to prepare for his trip into

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:56.900
the past.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.140
After he's arrested, Reese tells the police
psychologist that John Connor's forces found

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.580
a time machine in a lab complex they captured.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.260
Reese was sent to the 20th century, and the
lab complex was destroyed.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.180
Even if they were hustling to get to the past,
though which seems like a weird concern when

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.159
you have a time machine shouldn't John Connor
have taken a couple of minutes to remind Reese

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:19.250
that it was probably going to be tough to
protect his mom if she thought he was completely

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:19.820
insane?

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:24.080
The first conversation the two characters
have after Reese storms into a nightclub blasting

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:29.209
at a gun-toting assailant, which to put it
mildly is a pretty stressful situation, involves

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:33.760
Reese overloading Sarah with a flood of ridiculously
precise information about the Terminator.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.140
It's like he's reading the instruction manual,
going on about its microprocessor-controlled

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:39.840
hyperalloy combat chassis.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.560
That's not information that Sarah would care
about or even understand in the best of times,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.660
let alone when it's being shouted at her by
a sweaty man with a shotgun.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.020
Her initial reaction?

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.920
Once Reese pulls into a parking garage, Sarah
understandably tries to escape, biting Reese's

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:55.380
hand hard enough to draw blood.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:56.500
Can you blame her?

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.260
Reese is from a bleak, apocalyptic future
filled with killer robots, so the world of

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.900
1984 is probably as weird to him as he is
to Sarah, but someone definitely should've

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.180
given him some tips on how to start up a conversation
with another human being.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.800
And even if they didn't, Reese is a smart
guy.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.860
Starting off slow and then easing Sarah Connor
into the world of the "hyperalloy combat chassis"

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.900
would seem like a pretty obvious strategy.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:23.180
As you may already know, the Terminator is
pretty difficult to destroy.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:25.520
"It doesn't feel pity.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.820
Or remorse, or fear.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.640
And it absolutely will not stop, ever!"

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.380
For the most part, the best Kyle Reese can
do is slow the Terminator down, but as the

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:38.800
film progresses, the cyborg shows more and
more signs of battle damage.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.180
Eventually he's completely stripped of all
flesh, revealing his mechanical interior.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.560
The first casualties of the Terminator's outward
human disguise are his eyebrows, which are

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.940
burned off in an explosion after he attacks
Sarah and Reese in the nightclub.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.940
It makes sense that he'd lose them, too, since
they'd naturally be singed off with that kind

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:55.440
of fire.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:00.100
What doesn't make sense is that the eyebrows
are all the Terminator loses in that fight.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.600
That same exploding gas tank that engulfs
his entire body and completely erases his

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.080
eyebrows has done absolutely nothing to the
hair on his head, nor do his clothes seem

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.540
at all damaged by the flames.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.800
You could maybe write off the clothes by assuming
that the punk the Terminator stole his outfit

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:19.640
from invested in a fireproof wardrobe for
some unexplained reason, but for the hair,

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:20.820
there's no excuse.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.940
Even stranger, this scene happens right before
Reese has a conversation with Sarah where

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:29.300
he explicitly tells her that this model of
Terminator has fully organic skin and hair

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.860
covering up its metal skeleton.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.640
If those eyebrows are going, Arnold's perfect
coif should be dust, too.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.500
After showing up in 1984 and eventually finding
a pair of pants, Reese tries to explain the

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.020
future to Sarah as best as he can.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:47.060
He covers the emergence of Skynet and the
resulting nuclear holocaust, the rise of the

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.980
Terminators, and their grim plans for humanity.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.090
He explains it all, but the audience is left
with a mystery: how does Reese, or John Connor,

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:58.410
or anyone else in the future even know about
Skynet or how their world came under the literal

00:05:58.410 --> 00:05:59.780
iron fist of the machines?

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:03.940
Obviously, the survivors would know that the
machines were in charge, but when you think

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:07.840
about it, it isn't quite so obvious to figure
out how the human resistance ever learned

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.440
about the creation of Skynet.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.660
From what Reese tells us, Skynet decided the
fate of the human race "in a microsecond."

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.040
All of that presumably took place within the
A.I.'s processors.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:21.240
With the human race left scattered and without
access to technology or the power to make

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:25.750
that technology work, how would anyone be
able to access the data necessary to determine

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.440
how the world went sideways?

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:31.320
They don't necessarily need to know in order
to fight back, but they certainly need a way

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.620
to discover it if they're going to go back
in time and explain it to Sarah Connor.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.220
The big twist of The Terminator is, of course,
that it's all one big loop.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.640
After traveling back in time at the request
of John Connor, Reese and Sarah get it on,

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:47.870
and in the process, they conceive that same
John Connor, humanity's savior, who wouldn't

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.020
exist if it wasn't for the mission that he
ordered.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.480
That's destiny, baby!

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:55.640
Even if you can get your head around all the
time travel hijinx, though, the fact that

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.940
their brief affair even happens is just a
little weird, if not majorly weird.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.860
While they're on the run from the Terminator,
Reese ends up confessing to Sarah that her

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.000
unborn son gave him a picture of her, which
he kept and obsessed over for years.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.620
He tells her that he fell in love with her
because of that photo, without ever meeting

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.740
her or talking to her.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.340
It's more than just a little creepy.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.030
Sarah is obviously still reeling from being
hunted by a killer cyborg from the future,

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:23.160
not to mention being on the run from the authorities
and having her roommate murdered.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:27.440
And yet, even with all that, learning that
this sweaty weirdo became obsessed with her

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.420
thanks to a photograph that she hasn't even
taken yet is the weirdest thing that's happened

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:31.920
to her.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:33.780
Why doesn't she freak out even more?

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.280
Or, maybe she's into it?

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.120
We're not judging.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:40.280
It just seems like most first dates that start
off with, "I'm from the future," are already

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.960
going to be in enough jeopardy without adding,
"And I used to just stare at your pictures

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.080
for hours on end."

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:50.380
When he's being interviewed by the condescending
police psychologist, Reese says that things

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:53.800
in the future are actually starting to look
up for the few remaining humans.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.800
Under the leadership of John Connor, they
had won the battle against Skynet, and smashed

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.060
the machine's defense grid.

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:03.180
Sending the Terminator back in time was a
last ditch effort on the machines' part, made

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.520
out of desperation.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.360
"Why this elaborate scheme with the terminator?"

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.380
"It had no choice.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:11.900
Their defense grid was smashed.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:12.800
We’d won."

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:17.100
That's good news for humanity, but from what
little we see of the future, it's kind of

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.020
hard to believe.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:22.380
In both scenes we see of the future first
with Reese and his fellow soldiers being defeated

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:26.720
by a flying machine, and then with a Terminator
infiltrating an underground bunker and killing

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.360
almost everyone in sight the humans seem absolutely
hopeless.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.000
Maybe that's just how things are on the front,
but all of those piles of skulls aren't exactly

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:38.920
cheery, especially since Reese has to throw
everything he can at the Terminator to barely

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.920
slow it down, and eventually dies in the process.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.680
So how exactly does John Connor manage to
defeat this unstoppable, self-replicating

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:47.180
enemy?

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.160
The humans have barely any resources, and
we see them hunt rats for food.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:54.780
The fact that they have any weaponry at all
seems like at least a minor miracle.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:58.780
It's hard to understand how they could possibly
win, no matter how good John is at looking

00:08:58.780 --> 00:08:59.780
through binoculars.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:04.640
The Terminator ends with Sarah Connor in Mexico,
on her way to becoming the hardened badass

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:07.500
we find in 1991's Terminator 2: Judgment Day.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.040
She's got a new dog with her, presumably to
detect any other Terminators who might travel

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:16.250
through time, and she's recording a sort of
journal on tape as she travels, musing on

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:20.540
the question of whether or not she will tell
her unborn son that Kyle Reese was his father.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:24.680
Of all the things she could be telling someone,
anyone, about the future, is the identity

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.120
of John's father really her biggest concern?

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:31.200
A time-traveling paternity test might make
a pretty great episode for Maury Povich once

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.500
his show starts up in 1991, come on, Sarah.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.100
Let's get our priorities straight here.

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:39.500
Of course, that's not to suggest Sarah should
be yelling from the highest mountain that

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.080
everyone's going to die soon thanks to killer
robots.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.220
As we see in Terminator 2, that's exactly
the sort of thing that lands her in a mental

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:46.780
institution.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.740
It's just that in terms of what she should
reveal about the future, she's rating "do

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:54.800
I tell my son about his dad?" over "do I tell
people they should start building robot-proof

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:55.300
shelters?"

